      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. 
Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your ceilings, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, around the attic access hatch, around most windows, around the chimney block in the basement and along the rim joist of the floor on the North West side of the house. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

I expect that the air leakage around windows would be resolved once the window upgrades that you mentioned have been performed.

When the furnace is upgraded to a high performance sealed combustion unit, the chimney can be sealed off permanently to rectify the air leakage at this spot.

Air leakage was was not observed through the thermal camera on the bathroom exhaust fan and it was not possible to inspect this manually due to the ceiling height however when it comes time to replace the exhaust fan I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

The attic access hatch and ceiling electrical penetrations had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch as well as the ceiling electrical penetrations.. 

Savings: For a minimum 14.4% improvement you could potentially save $ 270 per year in energy costs, There are currently no rebates available for air sealing improvements from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1,300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. 
An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional. 



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 3.5inches (R10) of fiberglass batt insulation( due to age related detioration). I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home along with R-12 of Batt insulation.

I recommend adding at least 4 inches of XPS to maximise the insulation on the walls as it is not possible to add more than 3.5" of batt to the stud cavity without replacing the wall studs which would not yeild a reasonable return on investment.

Savings:
By adding R20 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 900 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to$ 2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.
</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>
The attic of your house was assumed to have R28 of blown mineral fibre insulation. As you mentioned you may be interested in adding more insulation, I have modelled adding an additional  R-28 of mineral fibre insulation for a total nominal R value of R 56.
Savings:
By increasing the insulation to R56, you could potentially save $150 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 125 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of R-50. The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for increasing home insulation in attics, cathedral ceilings and flat roofs. The amount you are eligible for will depend on factors such as what insulation is currently in your home, how much insulation you add and type of attic/ceiling your home has. Insulation slows the rate of heat loss, resulting in improved energy use and can help save money. Installing insulation in an attic, cathedral ceiling or flat roof is eligible for a grant, as long as minimum levels of insulation and coverage are achieved. If your house consists of more than one roof or roof type, the grant amount will be pro-rated or calculated based on roof type and area.
</ceilings>
                <foundation>
This section refers to the wall upgrade which I have extended down to include the header space which is considered part of the foundation.

Please refer to the comments regarding the wall upgrade for this.</foundation>
                <windows>
As some of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass and the ones below grade appear to be single paned windows with outer storm windows that are very leaky, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows for the main floor and hinged windows for the basement. 

Savings: 
By replacing the 15 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 700 per year in energy costs.  There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.
</windows>
                <doors>
I observed that your back door appears to be warped and does not close properly, I have modelled replacing both doors with  ENERGY STARÂ® qualified doors rated for climate zone 3.

Savings:
Replacing your both doors with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified door rated for climate zone 3, unfortunately, results in no substantial savings. 


There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>
It is possible for ventilation related problems such as stale air and condensation on windows to arise once the air sealing upgrades are performed on your home, I am including this section as a guideline should these problems arise.

Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.

Prior to considering an HRV there are a couple of other upgrades you could consider should you run into ventilation related problems,

You could operate the bathroom fan and rangehood more frequently and consider timer switches for the bathroom fan.

You could also consider installing a fresh air supply to the return air plenum of the furnace to aid in introducing fresh air to the interior of the house.

Should you decide that an HRV is necessary, I recommend that you have an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. An HRV could be tied into the existing furnace duct work to minimize installation requirements.

Savings:
By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV, you could potentially incur an additional expense of $200 per year in energy costs, however the additional comfort and better air quality may be worthwhile. (especially since there is a young child occupying the home). 

There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>
You currently have a new Moffat 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. 

Should you choose to replace this system at some point I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.

This would work in tandem with the proposed furnace conversion from oil to gas.

Savings:
By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save $ 1000 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 
</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83.4% AFUE.  I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE. 

Savings:
By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 97% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1750 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.


I have also modeled using a wood pellet stove to heat the basement floor area 50% of the time.

I recommend you install a ULC Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct.

Savings:
By a Certified pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 510 per year in energy savings, and qualify for up to 1/2 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove to a maximum of $ 2,000, from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

The two upgrades combined would result in a total of approximately $2260 in cost savings per year.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.
</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 

Since you expressed interest in looking at the possible savings on solar panels I have modeled a 5kW system and its associated savings below.

Savings:
By installing a 5 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially save $ 1750 per year in energy costs. 

There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program for electricity generation systems installed in a Hydro Community, including Yellowknife, however there are incentives available from the Canada Greener Homes Program

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a photovoltaic solar panel. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards.
</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>
The existing toilet seems to function properly and does not require an upgrade at the moment . However,when it is time to replace your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 

Savings:
By replacing your toilet(s) with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT 
Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020. 
Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines. 

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION 
For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit. 
Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include: 
1. In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report 
2. If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant. 
3. Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit 
4. All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional 
5. It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that: 
- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional* 
- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards 
- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home *When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory. 
6. All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada. 

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES 
You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure. 
Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights Â Hydro Community
Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.
Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 
For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass
The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.4 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.
During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation
Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:
        - a 158 CFM range hood   
        - one bathroom fan
        - a clothes dryer

You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FUEL COSTS
The following Dec. 1, 2022 values, excluding GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.13 min, $0.32 per kWh
Oil - $1.24 per liter 
Propane - $0.75 per liter delivered
Wood - $410 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $5.79 per bag, $351.29 per tonne bulk delivered

** Oil &amp; Biomass fuels in Yellowknife tend to experience cyclical fuel price volatility. The Arctic Energy Alliance therefore uses 5-year rolling average for these values to better help you evaluate longer term heating appliance purchase/upgrade decisions.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

